1
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:05,090
Look, I know you guys don't want to do this,

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:06,230
but we have no choice.

3
00:00:06,230 --> 00:00:07,600
So, you can either bitch and whine

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,100
or we can just get it over with.

5
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,000
I got "whine."

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,690
I got the "b word."

7
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:14,570
Yeah, well, it's in our contract

8
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:16,110
to serve on a university committee.

9
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:17,680
And frankly, this is one I believe in.

10
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:18,690
Okay, here we go.

11
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:21,790
Encouraging more women to pursue a career in the sciences.

12
00:00:21,790 --> 00:00:23,020
Come on, if I was any good

13
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,400
at convincing women to do stuff,

14
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:27,430
I wouldn't have spent so much of my 20s in the shower.

15
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:30,110
If you ask me,

16
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:32,260
this whole thing is a waste of our time.

17
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:33,390
Helping women?

18
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:35,400
Yeah, helping anyone.

19
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:38,490
People should take care of themselves.

20
00:00:38,490 --> 00:00:39,980
Oh, like yesterday,

21
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:42,270
when you made me drive you to the dry cleaners,

22
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:44,180
the pharmacy and the post office?

23
00:00:44,210 --> 00:00:47,560
I'm not saying people can't use tools.

24
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,090
Even an otter picks up a rock when he wants a clam.

25
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:53,960
You don't think it's worthwhile

26
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:56,380
to try to get more women working in science.

27
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,750
I think that's incredibly sexist of you.

28
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,130
I believe in a gender-blind society

29
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:03,630
like in Star Trek, where women and men

30
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:07,560
of all races and creeds work side by side as equals.

31
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:09,830
You mean where they were advanced enough

32
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:11,830
to invent an interstellar warp drive,

33
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:15,660
but a black lady still answered the space phone?

34
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,680
Oh, I did spend a lot of my shower time

35
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:19,760
with Lieutenant Uhura.

36
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:25,060
Howard's disturbing recollections aside,

37
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:26,880
I don't appreciate being forced to do

38
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,340
banal committee work.

39
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:29,960
Yes, I know, you're too smart for this.

40
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:30,920
Exactly.

41
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:32,550
It's like asking the Human Torch

42
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:34,550
to heat up your frozen burrito.

43
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,360
Got it.

44
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:37,470
All right, I'm thinking one way

45
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:39,880
to counter bias in the peer-review process,

46
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:42,690
is for papers to be submitted under gender-neutral names.

47
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,130
Like S. Smith

48
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:45,360
instead of Samantha Smith.

49
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:48,130
I suppose there is a history of professional women

50
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:50,800
using their initials, so as not to be pre-judged.

51
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:53,250
Harry Potter's J.K. Rowling,

52
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:55,200
uh, Star Trek's D.C. Fontana.

53
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,970
Van Nuys pole-dancer D.D. Melons.

54
00:02:01,170 --> 00:02:02,940
All right, I think we've really helped women today.

55
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:04,400
Let's fire up the old Xbox.

56
00:02:04,570 --> 00:02:06,970
Guys, please don't make this a school project

57
00:02:06,970 --> 00:02:08,450
where I'm the smart kid doing all the work

58
00:02:08,450 --> 00:02:10,190
while the slackers sit back and watch.

59
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:11,020
No, we're not.

60
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:13,070
This time you're the smart kid doing all the work

61
00:02:13,090 --> 00:02:15,930
while the even smarter kids sit back and watch.

62
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:18,900
So, you think I'm one of the smarter kids?

63
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,720
No, you're a tool I was using to make my point.

64
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:53,850
Guys, our topic is "Encouraging Women in Science."

65
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:56,870
Can you at least play a less sexist game?

66
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:03,260
How is it sexist?

67
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:06,400
My character wields a battle axe as well as any man.

68
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:09,630
Not to mention she has mammary glands

69
00:03:09,630 --> 00:03:11,770
that could nurse a family of 30.

70
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:14,620
And have enough milk left over

71
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:16,850
to open a Baskin Robbins.

72
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:21,670
Mother, warrior-princess, franchise owner--

73
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:24,750
I hear glass ceilings shattering all over town.

74
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:26,650
Sheldon, you're always saying

75
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:28,310
how much smarter you are than me.

76
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:30,570
Spend five seconds and come up with one idea

77
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:33,590
on how to get more women into science.

78
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:37,810
All your ideas address the issue at a university level.

79
00:03:37,810 --> 00:03:38,870
By then it's too late.

80
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:40,690
You need to design an outreach program

81
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:42,610
that targets girls at the middle school level

82
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:44,480
and sets them on an academic track

83
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:45,950
towards the hard sciences.

84
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:47,790
That's actually good.

85
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:49,470
Why didn't I think of that?

86
00:03:50,190 --> 00:03:52,790
Some people are otters, some people are rocks.

87
00:03:56,450 --> 00:03:57,610
I wonder if there's a way

88
00:03:57,610 --> 00:04:00,200
we could give the idea a trial run.

89
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:03,740
Maybe I could call my old middle school,

90
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:06,290
see if we can talk to some of the female students.

91
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:07,750
That's great-- try to set up something

92
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:09,180
for the three of us to go over there.

93
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:10,330
Oh, hold on.

94
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:12,120
While I'm comfortable speaking about science,

95
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:15,180
I'm not sure I know how to spark the interest of schoolchildren.

96
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:16,930
Better Google it.

97
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:20,020
What exactly are you looking up?

98
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:24,090
"How do I get 12-year-old girls excited."

99
00:04:24,090 --> 00:04:25,650
No! No!

100
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:33,400
Thanks again for letting me crash "girls' night."

101
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:34,250
Are you kidding?

102
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:36,380
You brought fancy wine and made fondue.

103
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:38,900
I've slept with guys for less.

104
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:45,460
It's a joke.

105
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:47,970
Based on real events.

106
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:50,980
Anyway, I was hoping I could, uh,

107
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:52,100
pick your brains a little.

108
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:54,010
I'm supposed to take Lucy out Friday

109
00:04:54,010 --> 00:04:56,360
and I need a killer first date.

110
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:59,220
Well, evolutionary biology says that women are attracted

111
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:01,340
to a man who is steady in the face of danger,

112
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:04,630
so I recommend an unsafe environment.

113
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:08,950
Seedy bar on the wrong side of the tracks...

114
00:05:09,430 --> 00:05:11,770
picnic near a lunatic asylum...

115
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:15,280
a wine tasting on Skid Row.

116
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:20,050
Eh, Lucy has some, uh, social anxiety issues.

117
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:22,490
Maybe we could start with something simpler.

118
00:05:22,490 --> 00:05:24,310
Why don't you take her to Disneyland?

119
00:05:24,310 --> 00:05:25,660
You go on Space Mountain,

120
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:27,700
you're in the dark, she's holding onto you.

121
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:28,840
Yeah, but you just have to remember,

122
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,150
that ride is shorter than you think

123
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:31,460
and they take a picture of you at the end,

124
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:33,920
so make sure you got your clothes back on.

125
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:38,630
It's a joke.

126
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:41,510
Based on real events.

127
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:43,800
Mm, Disneyland? I don't know.

128
00:05:43,870 --> 00:05:46,150
With all the crowds and the weird characters walking around,

129
00:05:46,150 --> 00:05:48,830
just reminds me too much of India.

130
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:51,680
I haven't been to Disneyland since I was a kid.

131
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:53,300
We should definitely go one weekend.

132
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:54,760
Weekends are too crowded.

133
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:56,520
So, blow off work, go on a weekday.

134
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:57,810
Hooky?

135
00:05:57,810 --> 00:05:59,990
I've never played hooky in my life.

136
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:01,280
My mom said that's how girls end up

137
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:04,030
addicted to reefer and jazz music.

138
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:08,690
It's more like how girls end up at a Best Western hotel

139
00:06:08,690 --> 00:06:11,220
with a 34-year-old guy named Luther.

140
00:06:13,390 --> 00:06:14,590
Joke?

141
00:06:14,590 --> 00:06:16,960
I can laugh about it now.

142
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:21,060
So, what do you say?

143
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:23,210
This Friday we ditch work and go to Disneyland?

144
00:06:23,210 --> 00:06:24,390
I'm in.

145
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:25,680
Me, too.

146
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:27,050
Excuse me,

147
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:28,980
I thought we were trying to solve my problem?

148
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:30,650
Oh, yeah, right.

149
00:06:30,650 --> 00:06:32,380
Wait, what was your problem again?

150
00:06:33,170 --> 00:06:35,440
I am a man who can't talk to women,

151
00:06:35,450 --> 00:06:36,320
trying to figure out

152
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,310
how to go on a romantic date with a girl

153
00:06:38,310 --> 00:06:41,080
who suffers from such crippling social anxiety

154
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:43,060
she can't be around other people.

155
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:45,850
Yeah, that's a toughie.

156
00:06:48,070 --> 00:06:49,550
Let's think.

157
00:06:55,630 --> 00:06:58,690
We doing just Disney or California Adventure, too?

158
00:07:01,770 --> 00:07:03,610
It's nice of your old school to let us try out

159
00:07:03,610 --> 00:07:05,750
our science talk on some female students.

160
00:07:05,750 --> 00:07:07,150
Well, they're actually pretty excited.

161
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:09,350
I'm their most famous alum.

162
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:10,690
If you don't count the serial killer

163
00:07:10,690 --> 00:07:12,390
who ate all those prostitutes.

164
00:07:14,530 --> 00:07:15,960
This must feel pretty good for you,

165
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:18,330
coming back to your alma mater as an astronaut.

166
00:07:18,330 --> 00:07:20,090
Yeah, last time I was here,

167
00:07:20,090 --> 00:07:22,250
I was just a scrawny little nerd.

168
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:26,160
And now you're also an astronaut.

169
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:28,400
So many memories.

170
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:30,520
I mean, how many times in these hallways

171
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:34,570
was I tripped, punched and spit on?

172
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:36,750
Oh, look, here's my old locker.

173
00:07:36,750 --> 00:07:39,420
I have a masters in engineering

174
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:43,060
and I still can't figure out how Scott Kapinski

175
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:45,400
got me and my briefcase to fit in there.

176
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:51,820
What?

177
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:53,640
Nothing.

178
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,580
Smart. We don't want any problems.

179
00:08:00,950 --> 00:08:03,390
I can't believe I ditched work for Disneyland.

180
00:08:03,390 --> 00:08:04,930
What did you tell your boss?

181
00:08:04,930 --> 00:08:07,390
Oh, I was very clever, I did it in stages.

182
00:08:07,390 --> 00:08:08,540
At 7:00 last night,

183
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:10,030
I called about a problem at the lab

184
00:08:10,030 --> 00:08:13,100
and casually mentioned I was going out for seafood.

185
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:14,750
At 9:30,

186
00:08:14,750 --> 00:08:17,970
I called and told him that one of my scallops tasted weird.

187
00:08:17,970 --> 00:08:19,420
At 11:30, I called and said

188
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:21,840
I was throwing up like a fire hose.

189
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:24,270
At 12:45,

190
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:27,160
I called and made nothing but dry heaving sounds,

191
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:29,770
and now I'm going to Disneyland.

192
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:33,040
Penny, what did you say?

193
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:34,390
I work at the Cheesecake Factory,

194
00:08:34,390 --> 00:08:36,170
I said "Bye."

195
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,100
So, what are we gonna do first?

196
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:40,950
I don't know about you guys,

197
00:08:40,950 --> 00:08:42,430
but I'm gonna make a beeline for the place

198
00:08:42,430 --> 00:08:44,400
that gives you a princess makeover.

199
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:45,900
Ooh, that sounds like fun.

200
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:46,860
You're kidding, right?

201
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:49,540
We're not just gonna get drunk and go on rides?

202
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:52,480
Come on, do it with us.

203
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:54,280
All right, whatever.

204
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:55,360
How does it work?

205
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:56,900
Okay, so, you pick your princess--

206
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:59,140
Sleeping Beauty, Snow White, Cinderella--

207
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:01,750
they give you hair, makeup, the works.

208
00:09:01,750 --> 00:09:04,180
Guess it would be fun to be Cinderella.

209
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:06,080
- Oh, I want to be Cinderella, too. - Yeah.

210
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:09,330
We can't all be Cinderella.

211
00:09:12,310 --> 00:09:13,610
Then how do we decide?

212
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:14,740
Well, it's simple.

213
00:09:14,750 --> 00:09:16,010
This was my idea.

214
00:09:16,010 --> 00:09:17,050
I'm driving.

215
00:09:17,050 --> 00:09:17,950
I'm Cinderella.

216
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:19,520
You bitches got a problem with that,

217
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:21,720
we could stop the car right now.

218
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:37,900
Excuse me, I'm meeting a girl here.

219
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:39,170
It's kind of our first date.

220
00:09:39,170 --> 00:09:41,620
In a library?

221
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:46,190
She and I are both a little awkward in social situations,

222
00:09:46,190 --> 00:09:48,580
so this seemed like a good idea.

223
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:52,350
People say I'm a little awkward, too.

224
00:09:52,990 --> 00:09:54,840
May I join you?

225
00:09:56,110 --> 00:09:58,020
No, you can't join us.

226
00:09:58,020 --> 00:09:59,940
Just go-go climb back up

227
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:02,290
whatever beanstalk you came down from.

228
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:09,640
You can do better.

229
00:10:14,170 --> 00:10:16,210
Oh, we're eating here?

230
00:10:22,290 --> 00:10:24,600
We're having a texting date?

231
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:30,590
I love that.

232
00:10:34,410 --> 00:10:36,970
As you're reading, it will help to remember

233
00:10:36,970 --> 00:10:39,580
I have an adorable accent.

234
00:10:43,250 --> 00:10:47,900
Okay, who's ready for some science?

235
00:10:51,330 --> 00:10:53,290
Me, too.

236
00:10:53,290 --> 00:10:55,610
Okay, I am Dr. Leonard Hofstadter.

237
00:10:55,610 --> 00:10:57,610
I am here with my friends Dr. Cooper

238
00:10:57,610 --> 00:11:01,120
and real-life astronaut Howard Wolowitz,

239
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:02,490
and we are going to show you girls

240
00:11:02,490 --> 00:11:06,150
how cool a job in science can be.

241
00:11:06,150 --> 00:11:07,590
How cool, you ask?

242
00:11:07,590 --> 00:11:10,700
Well, how about negative 273 degrees,

243
00:11:10,700 --> 00:11:14,560
'cause that's the temperature at which entropy reaches its minimum value.

244
00:11:17,810 --> 00:11:20,200
Did I just learn something new and have fun doing it?

245
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:22,050
What?

246
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:27,180
All right!

247
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:31,210
So now let's bring out theoretical physicist

248
00:11:31,210 --> 00:11:34,620
Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

249
00:11:39,250 --> 00:11:41,390
Hello, female children.

250
00:11:42,610 --> 00:11:45,300
Allow me to inspire you

251
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:49,030
with a story about a great female scientist.

252
00:11:49,030 --> 00:11:52,580
Polish-born, French-educated

253
00:11:52,580 --> 00:11:54,000
Madame Curie.

254
00:11:54,010 --> 00:11:56,880
Codiscoverer of radioactivity,

255
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:59,340
she was a hero of science...

256
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:01,680
until her hair fell out;

257
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:05,080
her vomit and stool became filled with blood...

258
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:09,660
and she was poisoned to death by her own discovery.

259
00:12:10,770 --> 00:12:12,860
With a little hard work,

260
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:16,470
I see no reason why that can't happen to any of you.

261
00:12:20,350 --> 00:12:21,640
Are we done? Can we go?

262
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:29,400
The thing to remember is

263
00:12:29,450 --> 00:12:31,650
you can go to outer space, too.

264
00:12:31,650 --> 00:12:33,510
I mean, look at me.

265
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:35,880
I went to this very school.

266
00:12:35,890 --> 00:12:38,290
Those desks you're sitting in,

267
00:12:38,290 --> 00:12:41,030
I was once superglued to one of them.

268
00:12:43,450 --> 00:12:45,160
Did you go to the moon?

269
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:47,020
No, but I did go to

270
